 "reading": "Not an edge, and not a near miss. A Sunday-reopen gap over half a 20-day ATR on the three US index CFDs did not fill before New York opened on Monday: the fade reached Friday's close in 11 of 102 trades in 2016-26 and 1 of 42 in 2008-15, most trades (79 of 102) ran out at 09:30 Monday, and the stream lost before any cost (pooled −0.095R a trade gross on 2016-26, −0.037R on 2008-15). After the index spread (about 0.024R) and one night of financing (about 0.007R) every market loses in both windows (month-sum t −1.28 to −1.69 on 2016-26), so no test meets any of the pre-registered conditions but n. The fill table says why: small gaps (under a quarter of the ATR) fill before Monday 09:30 in 76-81 % of weeks, but gaps of 0.5-1 ATR filled in 0 of 34 weeks in 2008-15 and 7 of 80 in 2016-26 (none of the up gaps): a large weekend gap on these indices is news being priced, not thin-book noise, and price tends to keep it. The real pooled mean (−0.126R) is below all 40 placebo runs on weekday evenings (p5 −0.060R, p95 +0.066R), so fading a big Sunday gap did worse than fading a same-size afternoon-to-evening move on an ordinary weekday. Never propose it as a council rule or filter; nothing live changed. Not tested (outside the pre-registration): a hold past 09:30 Monday, a partial-fill target, or trading with the gap."
